[[0,1390166,0,4,0,4,"2016-11-05T00:54:00","ibepokey","",[],"<p>We picked up a bottle of Sweet Mead at our local homebrew store, and my wife and mother-in-law LOVED it. Wife sent me a link to some cocoa-flavored mead, which i can't find locally (yet! my local liquor store says they can get anything by name.)</p>\n<p>So my wife found a website: <a href=\"http://gotmead.com\">http://gotmead.com</a>. found a recipe for Ancient Orange, Clove, and Cinnamon Mead. \r</p>\n<p>Went back to the store, picked up a 5 gal kit for Sack Sweet Mead, plus 3lb of Orange Blossom honey for the Ancient Orange stuff. I'm excited! I've done beer and wine kits but this is my first mead attempt.</p>","Anyone here ever make mead?","","Homebrewing"],[1,6733691,0,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T01:39:36","toats","",[],"<p>I haven't but I'm interested to hear how yours turns out. The orange clove and cinnamon recipe sounds delicious. I'm loathe to ruin a batch of precious honey!</p>"],[2,6733959,6733691,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T02:17:11","ibepokey","",[],"<p>from the looks of it, it's easier than wine, which is easier than beer.</p>\n<p>mix ingredients, pitch yeast, wait, xfer to secondary, wait, then bottle.</p>"],[1,6733774,0,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T01:51:24","raging_paranoia","",[],"<p>I highly recommend you find the Brewing Network podcast and/or the Beer Smith podcast with Michael Fairbrother of Moonlight Meadery. His advice about staggering yeast nutrients cut my mead brewing time in half.</p>\n<p>I recommend doing a copy of his &quot;Curt's Apple Pie&quot; which uses Apple juice, Madagascar vanilla beans, and Saigon cinnamon.</p>"],[2,6733947,6733774,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T02:15:51","ibepokey","",[],"<p>have you tried flavoring mead on the back end with cake flavorings? I've got plenty of Flavor West flavorings and was going to finish a batch off with a cocoa or Chocolate flavoring.</p>"],[1,6734086,0,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T02:32:37","Grospoliner","",[],"<p>Friend of mine did. Turned out great. Was honey, orange, and a vanilla stick.</p>"],[2,6734112,6734086,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T02:35:13","ibepokey","",[],"<p>my 1gal batch is that, plus raisins, a clove (or 2) and a pinch of nutmeg. </p>"],[1,6734403,0,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T03:17:01","NassTee","",[],"<p>I did Joe's Ancient Orange Mead. Turned out great, but it does take a long time.</p>"],[2,6734414,6734403,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T03:18:59","ibepokey","",[],"<p>they all take ~2 months, don't they? or do you mean small batches take longer?</p>"],[3,6734468,6734414,2,0,2,"2016-11-05T03:28:25","NassTee","[e]",[],"<p>JAOM takes longer than it would otherwise because you do things like putting in the oranges without removing the peel. That makes it take longer to age sufficiently than it would otherwise. I don't know exactly how long it would take otherwise, but it seemed to me that it took longer than I would normally expect it to, compared to other things I've brewed. I don't think the size of the batch really affects how long it takes, as long as you pitch a sufficient amount of yeast.</p>"],[4,6734483,6734468,1,0,1,"2016-11-05T03:31:01","ibepokey","",[],"<p>ah, ok, thanks for the clarification.</p>"]]
